class.description:
Assign a [member subtitle] and a [member sync_node] in the Inspector, then connect [signal segment_changed] to a [Label] or any other node that shows text. The player then follows the time of the sync node while it plays. The sync node can be an [AudioStreamPlayer], [AudioStreamPlayer2D], [AudioStreamPlayer3D], [VideoStreamPlayer] or [AnimationPlayer]. Playing, pausing, seeking and stopping the sync node is all the control you need.
If [member subtitle] is empty and the sync node is an audio or video player, the player picks the subtitle itself, in this order: the [code]subtitle_path[/code] of the player (for example [member AudioStreamPlayer.subtitle_path]), the [code]subtitle_path[/code] of the current stream (for an [AudioStreamPlaylist], the stream of the current track; it switches on [signal AudioStreamPlayer.track_changed], and a crossfade switches with [signal AudioStreamPlayer.stream_changed]), and a sidecar file next to the stream file with the same base name and the extension [code].srt[/code] or [code].vtt[/code] (for example [code]res://voice/intro.ogg[/code] uses [code]res://voice/intro.srt[/code]). The file is loaded with [ResourceLoader], so translation remaps apply, and it is loaded again only when the path changes. A [code]subtitle_path[/code] changed while the sync node plays applies when it starts playing again or changes its stream. [member SubtitlePlayback.subtitle] of [method get_playback] returns the subtitle in use. In the editor, the Inspector of the audio and video players has an [b]Add SubtitlePlayer[/b] button that adds a child [SubtitlePlayer] with [member sync_node] set to [code]..[/code].
Without a sync node, the player has a clock of its own: control it with [method play], [method seek] and [method stop], or start it with [member autoplay].
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
func _ready():
    $SubtitlePlayer.segment_changed.connect(_on_segment_changed)
    $Voice.play()

func _on_segment_changed(segment):
    $SubtitleLabel.text = SubtitleServer.get_display_text($SubtitlePlayer.subtitle, segment) if segment else ""
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
public override void _Ready()
{
    var player = GetNode<SubtitlePlayer>("SubtitlePlayer");
    var label = GetNode<Label>("SubtitleLabel");
    player.SegmentChanged += segment => label.Text = segment != null ? SubtitleServer.GetDisplayText(player.Subtitle, segment) : "";
    GetNode<AudioStreamPlayer>("Voice").Play();
}
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]
The player connects to [signal AudioStreamPlayer.playing_changed], [signal AudioStreamPlayer.stream_changed] and [signal AudioStreamPlayer.track_changed] of the sync node and reads its position every frame only while it plays (audio players: [method AudioStreamPlayer.get_track_position], so the time of the current playlist track). An [AnimationPlayer] is still polled every frame. The player updates in the process step, so it follows [member Node.process_mode] and stops while the tree is paused. It does not run in the editor. All of its logic is in a [SubtitlePlayback], which [method get_playback] returns.
[b]Note:[/b] When the sync node stops or finishes, the shown text is hidden. When it is paused ([member AudioStreamPlayer.stream_paused], [member VideoStreamPlayer.paused], or a paused scene tree), the text stays visible and the player stops advancing until it resumes; a stop while paused hides the text in the next frame.
